# Copy to .env and fill in your own values. Never commit .env.

# --- LLM provider (not used yet: retrieval works without a model) ---
LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic          # anthropic | openai | ollama

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=SET_YOUR_OWN
OPENAI_API_KEY=SET_YOUR_OWN

# Local model via Ollama needs no key.
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434

# --- Qdrant ---
# Default is embedded: a local folder, no server, no Docker, one process at a time.
# Nothing to set — the folder is anchored to the repo automatically.
#
# ⚠️ Only set QDRANT_PATH if you want a store somewhere else, and then make it an
# ABSOLUTE path. A relative value is resolved against the current working directory,
# so indexing from the repo root and querying from elsewhere would open two different
# folders — and the empty one answers with silence rather than an error. Leaving this
# unset avoids the whole problem.
# QDRANT_PATH=/absolute/path/to/qdrant_storage
#
# Set QDRANT_URL to use the container instead (docker compose up -d). It wins over
# QDRANT_PATH. The two stores are separate: what you indexed into the folder is not
# in the container.
# QDRANT_URL=http://localhost:6333

QDRANT_COLLECTION=tenders

# --- chunking: NOT environment variables. Pass them on the command line. ---
# 🔴 THIS BLOCK USED TO LIST CHUNK_MAX_CHARS, CHUNK_OVERLAP_CHARS AND TOP_K AS IF THEY
# WERE SETTINGS. NOTHING IN THE CODE HAS EVER READ THEM. Exporting them changed the
# behaviour not at all and reported nothing — so the first attempt to vary chunk size
# by editing this file would have produced an unchanged score and the wrong conclusion:
# "chunk size does not matter". It does. The knob is simply somewhere else.
#
# The real controls:
#   chunk size   ->  python scripts/index_pdf.py <pdf> --max-chars 1200   (default 1200)
#   preview it   ->  python scripts/show_chunks.py <pdf> --max-chars 1200
#   top_k        ->  python scripts/ask.py "..." --top-k 3   |   "top_k" in POST /ask
#   overlap      ->  min(OVERLAP_CHARS, max_chars // 4) in app/chunking.py — a constant,
#                    deliberately not tunable. 300 at the default, not the 500 this file
#                    used to advertise: the cap at a quarter of the chunk always applied.
#
# 🔑 WHY 1200: it is what scripts/index_pdf.py has always used, so the preview and the
# index now agree. It is NOT a size that fits the model. The embedding model reads only
# the first 128 tokens of a chunk — about 440 characters of Russian here, at 3.46
# characters per token — and discards the rest with no error. Measured on real text:
#   2000 chars -> up to 627 tokens -> every chunk truncated, ~26 % of each embedded
#   1200 chars -> up to 387 tokens -> every chunk truncated, ~43 % of each embedded
# Left alone on purpose, and now for a measured reason: rag-eval ran the same questions at
# 1200, 480 and 400 on a 13-page English paper. Searchable text went from 46 % to 97 % of
# the document and MRR did not move (0.3624 / 0.3408 / 0.3615). Smaller chunks fit the
# window and also triple the number of competing candidates; the effects cancelled.

# --- LLM provider: also not read yet. ---
# The keys above are placeholders for Week 3. There is no LLM client in this repo and
# POST /ask returns "mode": "retrieval-only" precisely so that stays true and checkable.
